<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Woodlark Island</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Muyua</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Woodlark Island, or Muyua, is an island in the Milne Bay Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papua-new-guinea">Papua New Guinea</a>, east of the mainland. A gold rush began there in the 1890s under British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a>, and the island is also known for its part in the kula exchange ring described by the anthropologist Bronisław Malinowski.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/woodlark-island.jpg" alt="Woodlark Island" width="300" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hobe / Holger Behr · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karta_PG_Woodlark_isl.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Woodlark Island is the main island of the Woodlark Islands archipelago in the Solomon Sea. The wider group also takes in Madau, Boagis, Nusam and Nanon to the west, Nubara to the east and the Marshall Bennett group to the south-west. Inhabitants of some other islands in the province call it Murua. The island has a volcanic core of Tertiary age and a wide belt of limestone formed mainly by corals, and coral reefs are still active around its shores.</p><p>A quarry at Tubukuia mountain was an important source of stone for Suloga axes, which were traded as far west as the Gulf of Papua. An extinct marine gharial, Gavialis papuensis, lived on the island in the Pleistocene or Holocene and was presumably hunted to extinction by the first human settlers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The island takes its English name from the Australian whaling ship Woodlark under Captain George Grimes, which called in the 1830s. Other whalers came for water and wood, and islanders sometimes served in their crews. In 1841 all but one of the surviving crew of the wrecked whaler Mary were killed there. The Pontifical Institute for Foreign Missions sent five priests and two brothers in 1852, and Giovanni Battista Mazzucconi was killed in 1855 by an islander named Avicoar who opposed the mission.</p><p>Richard Ede and Charles Lobb, traders from the nearby Laughlan Islands, found gold in 1895. In 1896–97 some 400 white miners and 1,600 Papuan labourers produced 20,000 ounces of gold. The anthropologist Fred Damon estimated that the population fell from about 2,200 at first European contact to between 700 and 900 by about 1915. On 30 June 1943, in Operation Chronicle, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> 112th Cavalry Regiment landed on Woodlark, and Seabees of the 60th Naval Construction Battalion built an airbase at Guasopa Bay.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The people speak Muyuw, an Austronesian language of the Kilivila–Louisiades group. An unofficial count in 2010 put the population at around 6,000, with the largest settlements at Kulumadau and Guasopa and the largest village at Kaulay. Ebony logging has been an important source of income since the 1970s, and a plan to turn 70% of the island over to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a> was dropped after islanders opposed it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Woodlark Island” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Small gold rushes on Misima and <b>Woodlark Island</b> drew prospectors.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-new-guinea">British New Guinea</a></li></ul></section>
